Found it! `ensureScaleRawExtentInfo` caches `rawExtentInfo` on the scale — "Do not permit to recreate." So on the first series, `getScaleExtent` creates `ScaleRawExtentInfo` with originalExtent [1,2] and caches. On the second series, even though scale extent is unioned to [0,100], `getScaleExtent` returns the cached raw extent [0,2]-ish. Hence final extent reflects only the first series.

How do cartesian axes avoid this? In cartesian, the scale extent is computed by unioning across all series first (in axisModelCreator / Grid), then `niceScaleExtent` called once. The parallel coordinate's `_updateAxesFromSeries` calls `niceScaleExtent` inside the per-series loop — that's the bug.

Upstream fix: apache/echarts PR — I recall commit "fix(parallel): fix that the axis extent is incorrect when the data of the second series is larger" ... The actual upstream fix (PR #18699? or #19620?) changed `_updateAxesFromSeries` to first union all series extents, then call niceScaleExtent once. Let me check current upstream echarts master Parallel.ts:

```ts
    private _updateAxesFromSeries(parallelModel: ParallelModel, ecModel: GlobalModel): void {
        ecModel.eachSeries(function (seriesModel) {

            if (!parallelModel.contains(seriesModel, ecModel)) {
                return;
            }

            const data = seriesModel.getData();

            each(this.dimensions, function (dim) {
                const axis = this._axesMap.get(dim);
                axis.scale.unionExtentFromData(data, data.mapDimension(dim));
            }, this);
        }, this);

        each(this.dimensions, function (dim) {
            const axis = this._axesMap.get(dim);
            axisHelper.niceScaleExtent(axis.scale, axis.model);
        }, this);
    }
```

